I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern range/quality, and the available crystallographic tooling first. Then I’ll identify candidate phases, refine a multiphase GSAS-II model, and validate that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and statistics all come from the saved `final.gpx`.

The chemical system is ZnO–WO₃ heated at 600 °C, so ZnWO₄ is a strong hypothesis, but I’ll treat residual oxides and polymorphs as candidates rather than assuming a single-phase product. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the local reference-search and GSAS-II interfaces.

Peak positions already show more complexity than a clean ZnWO₄ trace: the strong group near 23–24.4° and the 30.45/30.69° double maximum need explicit accounting. I found the filtered local COD index and am comparing all Zn–W–O, ZnO, and WO₃ reference structures against the observed pattern before choosing refinement phases.

The reference comparison resolves three crystalline phases: monoclinic wolframite ZnWO₄ (P2/c), monoclinic γ-WO₃ (P2₁/c), and wurtzite ZnO (P6₃mc). Their diagnostic non-overlapping peaks are all present. I’m moving into staged Rietveld refinement now, keeping atomic coordinates tied to validated reference structures while refining scale, background, lattice, and physically limited profile terms.

The first stable cycles give an excellent phase-level match (`Rwp` already ~5.4%) and provisional crystalline fractions near 71% ZnWO₄, 19% WO₃, and 10% ZnO. I also detected an unnecessary global-scale/phase-scale correlation in the initial project setup; after this trial completes I’ll remove that redundant degree of freedom and compare profile/background variants before finalizing uncertainties.

The initial fit’s phase assignment is secure, but its covariance was not: GSAS-II defaulted the overall histogram scale to “refine,” making it mathematically redundant with the three phase scales. I have fixed that scale and am testing reduced profile models; this should preserve the good fit while making the phase-fraction uncertainties meaningful and avoiding highly correlated, nonphysical width terms.

The reduced-model comparison is still running. The first variant is taking longer because each profile choice is being re-refined from a fresh project rather than inheriting parameters from the earlier, singular fit; this is deliberate so the comparison is unbiased.

The comparison converged on the same fractions across every profile choice (spread under 0.1 wt%), which is reassuring. It also exposed that the best reduced fit is effectively Lorentzian: its Gaussian variance was trying to cross below zero and being clipped internally. I’m replacing that boundary-hitting parameter with an explicit zero-Gaussian effective profile, then checking the profile residuals and the sensitivity to background order.

The boundary-free fits are running concurrently. So far the important scientific result is already stable: ZnWO₄ ≈68.7–68.8 wt%, WO₃ ≈20.2 wt%, and ZnO ≈11.0 wt% of the modeled crystalline material. The remaining work is model hygiene—selecting the least correlated profile/background description and ensuring every submitted artifact is regenerated from that exact saved project.

A 20-term smooth Chebyshev background gives the lowest residual without changing the composition materially; its characteristic variation is several degrees wide, so it cannot absorb the narrow Bragg peaks. Across 10, 15, and 20 terms, phase fractions vary by at most 0.07 wt%. I’m now driving the weak ZnO lattice terms to a stable endpoint and will use the model spread—not only the formal covariance—as the practical fraction uncertainty.

The final parameterization is now stable and nonsingular: the saved model converged with no SVD zeros, `Rwp = 3.747%`, `Rexp = 2.765%`, and GOF `= 1.358`. The weak ZnO lattice was optimized separately and then held fixed because its simultaneous cell shifts oscillated against overlapping peaks; the ZnO phase identity and scale remain secure, but its lattice precision is lower than the formal ZnWO₄/WO₃ precision. I’m exporting and cross-validating the requested deliverables from this exact project state.

Completed the three-phase GSAS-II refinement:

